<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Ladoga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Ladoga, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, is the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its basin linked the Baltic to the river routes of Rus' through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volkhov-river">Volkhov</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staraya-ladoga">Staraya Ladoga</a> on its southern shore was an early Viking-age trading town. During the siege of Leningrad in 1941–1944 the 'Road of Life' across its ice carried supplies into the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga.jpg" alt="Lake Ladoga" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yoneh at Finnish Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sortavalan_saaristoa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
